{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794514688,"nonce":"UePgoK4ScTcaZ54qwzH89QsS/5s=","title":"A ScrollRef command from another thread is not applied until an input event wakes the window","message":"ScrollRef::set_scroll_offset (and the other programmatic scroll commands) store a pending command and call a trigger that is a no-op: \"scroll operations are processed every frame by process_pending_scroll_refs\". The windowed runner sits in ControlFlow::Wait and runs a frame only when something calls window.request_redraw() or the scheduler's wake callback fires. A command set from a thread or timer while the window is idle is therefore applied on the next frame, which is the next mouse move.\n\nSeen with the scroll_bench example when it scrolled itself from its own thread: the list moved for the first couple of seconds, then froze with the commanded offset running on and the offset the runner reports back stuck (printed both; process_pending_scroll_refs stopped being called), and jumped when the pointer moved over it. A small flat list kept going only because something else kept frames coming.\n\nThe same symptom is documented in frame.rs for smooth-scroll commands that \"jump after the next mouse move\"; that fix covers a command that starts a spring in a frame already running, not one issued while idle.\n\nThe example now scrolls from AnimationScheduler::register_tick_callback, which keeps frames coming. Needed: a command from outside a frame should ask for one, for example by ScrollRef's trigger waking the loop the way a scheduler registration does (notify_active -\u003e wake_callback). Check the cost on the hot path: the trigger is a no-op on purpose so a scroll does not wake the reactive graph; waking the loop is not the reactive graph.","files":null}]}